2.5.m:
Śiva said:
This ruler of Tripura is indeed virtuous at present;
where virtue prevails, he must never be killed by the wise.

Sanatkumāra said:
Having heard these words, the gods fell into grief;
with their lotus-faces withered, they spoke again to Viṣṇu:

Sanatkumāra said:
Having spoken thus, the gods grieved again and again;
they found no rest nor could they proceed from the presence of the supreme deity.

Maheśvara said:
O bull among Dānavas, blessed are you, devoted to me and free from perturbations;
all those boons desired by you have now been granted to you.

Viṣṇu and the others said:
You must never fear at all, O shaven-headed ones;
this entire excellent act was done by the command of Śiva.

Sanatkumāra said:
Having spoken thus, [Brahmā] summoned Śukra and consecrated him to the kingship;
having taken leave of the lord of rivers, Brahmā vanished.

Hearing that, the blessed Rudra displayed vehement wrath;
he became terrifying and exceedingly dreadful, blazing through the quarters.

Thus ends the twentieth chapter, titled 'Description of the General Battle between the Attendants and the Asuras,' in the story of the slaying of Jalandhara, in the fifth Yuddhakhaṇḍa of the second Rudrasaṃhitā, in the glorious Śiva Mahāpurāṇa.

Hiraṇyakaśipu said:
O blessed Lord, ruler of creatures, O Grandfather, O god, let there never be fear of death for me
from weapons, missiles, nooses, thunderbolts, dried trees, great mountains, water, fire, or the strikes of enemies;

Saying nothing at all, the protector of the bhūtas tossed that foremost of poets into his mouth like a fruit;
and a great cry of distress—'Alas! Alas! Alas!'—was raised loudly by all those asuras.

Thus ends the forty-seventh chapter, entitled 'The Description of the Swallowing of Śukra in the War with Andhaka,' in the War Section, the fifth section of the second saṃhitā, the Rudrasaṃhitā, in the glorious Śiva Mahāpurāṇa.
